ntdll: start each program from a game bundle named after it.

sake starts a title from <engine>/SakeGame.app/Contents/MacOS/wine, a copy of Wine's
unix side inside an .app that declares LSApplicationCategoryType games, because macOS
decides Game Mode from the bundle a process's executable is in. Every process the title
starts afterwards comes from the same loader, since init_paths() derives the loader for
the next process from realpath() of ntdll.so -- so without this, Diablo IV started from
Battle.net is in that one bundle, and the Dock labels its tile with that bundle's name.
The Dock takes that label from the bundle's file name and nothing else: measured on
2026-09-23, neither CFBundleName nor an _kLSDisplayNameKey the process sets on itself
changes it.

So when SAKE_GAME_BUNDLES is set, a process being started whose loader sits in
<bundle>/Contents/MacOS is started from $SAKE_GAME_BUNDLES/<key>/<program>.app instead,
made on first use as a mirror of the parent's bundle: directories made, symlinks copied
as they are, files hard-linked, and an Info.plist of its own naming the program. Hard
links and not symlinks, because through a symlink realpath() would lead back into the
parent's bundle and the program's own children would leave its. They cost directory
entries only.

The name is extract_exe_name(), which CW HACK 22144 already uses for the same purpose:
that hack names a link after the program so the Dock shows it, and does so only when
WINEDLLPATH is set, in a temporary directory that is no bundle. This runs before it, and
when it has put the program in a bundle the hack is skipped: its link would take the
program back out, which with WINEDLLPATH set is what happened, measured on 2026-09-23.

<key> is a 64-bit FNV-1a of the exe's unix path, lower-cased, and the bundle identifier
is dev.typester.sake.program.<program>.<key>. The name alone is not the program: two
games may both ship a Launcher.exe or a crashpad_handler.exe, and a bundle is one
identity to macOS -- name, icon and Game Mode session -- so keyed by name the second
would have been shown with the first one's icon. Measured the same day with notepad.exe
and winemine.exe both copied in as Launcher.exe: two bundles, two identifiers, two
icons, and the same exe under another spelling of its path found its own. The extension
is cut here: extract_exe_name() measures the name without it and then returns the whole
string, which made the first bundles notepad.exe.app.

Measured on 2026-09-23 against a lab bundle and a fresh prefix, `wine start notepad`:
notepad came up from notepad.app as its own bundle identifier, Foreground, and explorer
from explorer.app as UIElement; the Dock labelled the tile "notepad", and full-screen
turned Game Mode on. The bundle's ntdll.so was the parent's inode.

The bundle also carries the program's own icon, because the Game Overlay shows the icon
a bundle had when macOS first registered it and nothing later: an icon added afterwards
stayed blank even through lsregister -f. So it has to be in place before the first exec,
and it is: the exe's first RT_GROUP_ICON and the RT_ICON images it lists, put back
together as Contents/Resources/AppIcon.ico and named in CFBundleIconFile. A .ico is
enough; the file is read through $WINEPREFIX/dosdevices, since image_path is the Windows
path. An exe with no icon, or one this cannot read, gets a bundle without one. Measured
on 2026-09-23: notepad.exe gave a ten-image .ico and the overlay showed notepad's icon.

A bundle is current when its loader is the same file as the running one and its
Info.plist carries SakeBundleFormat 2. One without the marker was made before the icon
was, and is made again under a new identifier, dev.typester.sake.program.<program>:
registered once without an icon, the old identifier would stay without it. One that is
not current -- the title's bundle was remade after the engine was rebuilt, or the format
is older -- is renamed aside to <key>/.stale-<pid> and made again, and a failed or lost
attempt leaves <key>/.staging-<pid>; sake sweeps both. Nothing changes when the variable
is unset, when the running loader is not in a bundle, or when any step fails: the
process is then started from the loader it would have been.

